  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 515 AM EDT Sat Jul 18 2026\n\nA shortwave trough moving through the cyclonic flow on the\neastern side of high amplitude ridging over the Plains and into\ncentral Canada. A cold front associated with this shortwave will\nbegin to push south into our area around midday. SBCAPE values\nof 2500-3000 J/kg will be available along with bulk shear \nvalues of 25 to around 50 kts. Surface dew points in the mid 70s\nwill provide ample moisture and PW values are around the 2 inch\nmark today. Storms today should have a fairly progressive speed\nso will help keep the flooding risk a bit lower than the last\ncouple of days when the steering flow was much slower. Helicity\nvalues are forecast to be only around 50 m2/s2 which will keep\nthe risk of tornadic activity low. However, with any boundary in\nclose proximity to storms there could be an isolated brief spin\nup or two. Lapse rates of 6.5-7.0 deg/km will help to allow for\nsome hail production however not ideal for large hail especially\nwith the very humid and warm lower levels but the melting of\nhail coupled with the heavier downpour potential will make\noutflow gusts the biggest threat with the storms today. SPC did\nadd a fairly small section of enhanced to our far eastern \nportions of the CWA in Henry and Putnam counties of Ohio with\nthe better dynamics further east of our area. However, the \nmajority of the area is currently under a Slight risk today. \n\nIn the wake of the cold front as it pushes southward a more\nnortherly flow will arrive and this will make for hazardous\nbeach conditions over the southeastern Lake Michigan and wave\nheights from 3 to 6 feet on the big lake. A Beach hazards \nstatement has been issued to address this threat as well as a\nSmall Craft Advisory. Drier airmass will also accompany the\nfrontal passage.\n\nSunday will be a bit cooler and drier with a more northwesterly\nflow aloft and high temperatures will be mainly in the low to \nmid 80s and dew points only in the 60s. We will also see a short\nbreak in precipitation chances on Sunday into Monday before\nanother trough pushes into the lower Great Lakes region on\nMonday evening. This will also bring another chance of showers\nand thunderstorms to the area. A few of the storms may become\nstrong to severe so will need to watch this in the coming days. \n\nA northwesterly flow aloft sets up again over the region by \nTuesday and remains through the rest of next week and this will\nhelp to keep temperatures slightly cooler to near normal values.",
